# Hand off to engineers

1. Ask the designer to confirm the prototype is finished and every screen has its loading, empty,
   error and success states. If a note is still pending, finish it first with `design-space-change`.
2. If the prototype proposes a change to a product component, make sure the proposal is described
   in the prototype and says which product component it replaces.
3. Run `npm run design-space:ready -- <name>` from the design space folder. It marks the prototype
   ready, saves the work, sends it, and opens a pull request.
4. If it refuses, read the reason to the designer in plain words:
   - "not on a proto branch": the work was not started with `design-space-new`.
   - "cannot be handed off by a designer": a listed file is outside the prototype folders, or is
     a test file. Each file shows the reason. Do not work around it; remove a file you added by
     mistake, otherwise ask an engineer.
   - "not ready yet": say which part of the prototype is missing and fix it.
5. When it succeeds, share the link it prints. Running it again after more changes updates the
   same pull request and prints "updated the existing pull request" with its link; no second one
   is opened.
6. Tell the designer the pull request now follows the team's usual review: whoever the team
   names reviews it, and it is merged once its checks pass and GitHub allows it. If the check
   fails, read its message in plain words and do not work around it; ask an engineer.

Merging hands the prototype to engineering. It waits as "ready for dev" until engineers move it
into the product, which they do when they are ready.

Next step: run `design-space-new` for the next prototype, or `design-space-help` for guidance.
